WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.530
ROGER MARTINEZ:
Last time, we talked

00:00:01.530 --> 00:00:03.072
about how you can
secure your service

00:00:03.072 --> 00:00:06.510
accounts by understanding the
IAM roles associated with them.

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:09.600
One of the roles I mentioned
was a service account key admin,

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:11.850
a role that lets you create
service account keys that

00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:13.500
could then be used
to allow users

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:15.090
to authenticate as
a service account

00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:17.070
from outside Google Cloud.

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:19.200
That sounds kind of
risky, doesn't it?

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:21.630
Today, I'll go over
a few fundamentals

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:24.960
of managing service account keys
so that you can reduce the risk

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:26.850
and keep your
service account safe.

00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:29.760
And in case you want to minimize
using service account keys,

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:31.600
I'll show you how,
in some cases,

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:33.450
you can authenticate
as a service account

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:36.630
without having to create
or distribute keys at all.

00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:37.350
Let's get to it.

00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:39.600
I'm going to tell you what's
what with service account

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:41.070
keys and impersonation.

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:45.444
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:51.110
Before we dive into
service account keys, let's

00:00:51.110 --> 00:00:54.620
talk about the security
implication of using them.

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:56.960
For users that already have
access to your project,

00:00:56.960 --> 00:00:58.940
there aren't any
special roles that they

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:02.540
need to use service account
keys to act as them.

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:05.870
That means that any user in
your project's IAM policy

00:01:05.870 --> 00:01:08.210
has access to that key
will be able to utilize

00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:10.820
the corresponding service
account to access APIs

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:12.960
and services.

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:14.720
This can be
especially problematic

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:17.810
if the service account has
broad access to your project.

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:20.130
And on top of that, once
you distribute a key,

00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:22.130
it can be difficult to
make sure that it doesn't

00:01:22.130 --> 00:01:24.560
fall into the wrong hands.

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:26.630
For this reason,
it's a best practice

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:29.690
to rotate keys as
often as possible.

00:01:29.690 --> 00:01:32.270
Rotating keys is a
process of swapping out

00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:36.380
old keys for new ones and then
deactivating the old ones.

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:38.640
It's a simple
three-step process.

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:41.960
First, you generate new keys for
each of your service accounts.

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:44.720
Then you replace the
old key with the new one

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:46.610
wherever it's being used.

00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:48.850
And last, you
delete the old ones

00:01:48.850 --> 00:01:51.060
so that they don't work anymore.

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:53.900
So, how do we generate
new service account keys?

00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:57.320
If you're using Google Cloud
Console or the G Cloud CLI,

00:01:57.320 --> 00:02:00.680
you have two ways of creating a
key for your service account--

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:05.000
uploading your own or having
Google generate it for you.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:06.650
If you want to
upload your own key,

00:02:06.650 --> 00:02:11.000
you'll use GCloud IAM service
accounts keys upload, followed

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:15.470
by the file with your public
key and the IAM account flag,

00:02:15.470 --> 00:02:18.290
to indicate the service
account you want to use it for.

00:02:18.290 --> 00:02:20.090
You will need to
upload the private key.

00:02:20.090 --> 00:02:23.090
It's up to you to
keep it secured.

00:02:23.090 --> 00:02:26.780
It will be used to access your
project as a service account.

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:28.850
To have Google generate
a new key pair,

00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:33.050
you'll use GCloud IAM
service accounts keys create

00:02:33.050 --> 00:02:34.910
followed by the
output file you want

00:02:34.910 --> 00:02:38.210
to save your private key
to and the IAM account

00:02:38.210 --> 00:02:41.780
flag to indicate the service
account you want the key for.

00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:44.090
This will immediately
save the private key

00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:45.710
to the file indicated.

00:02:45.710 --> 00:02:49.880
This will be the only time the
private key is provided to you.

00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:51.620
Remember, it's
your responsibility

00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:55.290
to keep the private
keys safe and secure.

00:02:55.290 --> 00:02:58.010
The output file, when you have
Google Cloud generate a key,

00:02:58.010 --> 00:02:59.880
will look like this.

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:04.100
It's a JSON object with fields
like project ID, private key ID

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:07.460
and private key itself, the
service account email and ID,

00:03:07.460 --> 00:03:09.020
and more.

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:11.570
Note that the key in
this format can be used

00:03:11.570 --> 00:03:13.610
for running GCloud commands.

00:03:13.610 --> 00:03:15.890
If you want to use a
key to access any cloud

00:03:15.890 --> 00:03:17.810
resources using
the REST API, you

00:03:17.810 --> 00:03:20.330
can generate a key by
sending an HTTP POST

00:03:20.330 --> 00:03:22.820
request to the
corresponding API URL,

00:03:22.820 --> 00:03:27.390
along with your access token
that will turn a private key.

00:03:27.390 --> 00:03:29.510
The format of the JSON
you get with this method

00:03:29.510 --> 00:03:30.832
is a bit different.

00:03:30.832 --> 00:03:33.290
We recommend that you just use
the same method you're going

00:03:33.290 --> 00:03:35.270
to use for future API calls.

00:03:35.270 --> 00:03:36.950
Otherwise, you have
to edit the key

00:03:36.950 --> 00:03:38.810
to match the desired format.

00:03:38.810 --> 00:03:42.260
When you want to delete an old
key, you'll need the keys ID,

00:03:42.260 --> 00:03:46.280
GCloud IAM service accounts keys
list, followed by the service

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:48.830
account will spit out the
keys that currently exists

00:03:48.830 --> 00:03:50.870
for that service
account by key ID,

00:03:50.870 --> 00:03:54.830
along with the date of creation
and the date of expiration.

00:03:54.830 --> 00:03:58.160
With that, you can delete
it with GCloud IAM service

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:01.100
accounts keys delete,
followed by the key ID

00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:03.230
from the previous output.

00:04:03.230 --> 00:04:06.110
The old key is now unusable to
authenticate as your service

00:04:06.110 --> 00:04:08.510
account, but if you replace
it in all the places

00:04:08.510 --> 00:04:12.170
that you need it to work,
that shouldn't be a problem.

00:04:12.170 --> 00:04:14.660
If you need a way for users
to access service accounts

00:04:14.660 --> 00:04:16.035
but you're concerned
about having

00:04:16.035 --> 00:04:19.190
to manage service account
keys, there is some good news.

00:04:19.190 --> 00:04:22.190
For some use cases, the
key really isn't required.

00:04:22.190 --> 00:04:25.220
Instead, users can create
short-lived service account

00:04:25.220 --> 00:04:26.660
credentials.

00:04:26.660 --> 00:04:28.580
Short-lived service
account credentials

00:04:28.580 --> 00:04:30.710
have a limited
lifetime, with durations

00:04:30.710 --> 00:04:33.200
of just a few hours or shorter.

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:35.900
They also generate less risk
than long-lived credentials,

00:04:35.900 --> 00:04:38.180
such as service account keys.

00:04:38.180 --> 00:04:41.330
A user of the service account
token creator role on a service

00:04:41.330 --> 00:04:42.920
account will be able
to take advantage

00:04:42.920 --> 00:04:45.080
of short-lived credentials.

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:47.990
For example, they can
generate an OAuth 2 access

00:04:47.990 --> 00:04:50.780
token for the service account
to access the Google Cloud REST

00:04:50.780 --> 00:04:53.270
API as a service account.

00:04:53.270 --> 00:04:55.065
Additionally, a
user of this role

00:04:55.065 --> 00:04:56.690
can use the impersonate
service account

00:04:56.690 --> 00:04:59.600
flag on any GCloud command
to run it as a service

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:02.150
account, no keys required.

00:05:02.150 --> 00:05:04.070
Depending on your
organizational needs,

00:05:04.070 --> 00:05:05.840
you could reduce,
or even eliminate,

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:08.510
the use of service account
keys, and in effect,

00:05:08.510 --> 00:05:12.680
reduce the attack
surface of your projects.

00:05:12.680 --> 00:05:14.810
By understanding
service account keys

00:05:14.810 --> 00:05:17.540
and implementing a solid
rotation process for them,

00:05:17.540 --> 00:05:20.150
you'll be taking some major
steps in securing your project

00:05:20.150 --> 00:05:21.650
service accounts.

00:05:21.650 --> 00:05:24.020
If you're able to cut
out keys where you can

00:05:24.020 --> 00:05:26.090
and take advantage of
short-lived service account

00:05:26.090 --> 00:05:29.108
credentials, then
that's even better.

00:05:29.108 --> 00:05:31.400
We've covered a lot of ground
in the last four episodes

00:05:31.400 --> 00:05:32.990
about service accounts.

00:05:32.990 --> 00:05:35.360
Hopefully, by now, you've
got a pretty good idea

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:37.700
of what they are and
how to best manage them

00:05:37.700 --> 00:05:39.800
in your Google Cloud projects.

00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:41.750
Next time, we'll
dive a little deeper

00:05:41.750 --> 00:05:43.280
in an extended
episode, where we'll

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:45.050
see service accounts in action.

00:05:45.050 --> 00:05:46.730
See you soon.

00:05:46.730 --> 00:05:49.780
[MUSIC PLAYING]

